During the writing process, teachers support **monitoring, guiding, intervening, and maintaining collaborative dynamics**. The evidence points to a teacher role that is less directive and more responsive, helping students stay on task while using technology to mediate collaboration and feedback. The higher-education systematic review emphasizes that successful technology-enhanced collaborative academic writing (TECAW) requires the intentional orchestration of technologies and instructional designs aligned with pedagogical goals at each stage of collaborative writing [N3-F2]. It also notes that platforms such as Google Docs, Moodle, Zoom, and WhatsApp can function as task-supporting tools that facilitate drafting, feedback, and revision [N3-F2]. This suggests that during writing, teachers use technology to support **drafting, real-time feedback, and ongoing revision**.

Technology mediates collaboration and feedback by providing shared writing spaces, real-time interaction, and structured feedback mechanisms. The systematic review identifies cloud-based word processors and shared documents as central to in-writing support, with Google Docs and similar tools enabling students to see each other’s ideas in real time [N3-F2][N3-R4]. The “5 Ways to Enrich Writing Workshops with Technology” blog adds that collaborative tools like Google Docs allow students to share work across classrooms and devices, which supports continuous collaboration [N2-R2]. The evidence also indicates that technology can support **collaborative revising and editing** and **peer review and feedback**, which are explicitly mapped as core instructional strategies during writing [N3-F2].

Examples of monitoring, guiding, and intervening during writing include:
- **monitoring progress** through shared documents and real-time collaboration [N3-F2][N3-R4];
- **guiding students** by using scaffolding and task-supporting tools to keep them focused on the writing task [N3-F2];
- **intervening when needed** by using peer feedback, teacher feedback, or structured revision prompts [N3-F2][N4-R1];
- **maintaining collaborative dynamics** by using platforms that support communication and idea co-construction [N3-F2].

In this phase, teacher practices are especially tied to **technology-mediated collaboration, real-time feedback, and task orchestration** [N3-F2][N2-R2]. The evidence is strongest for shared writing spaces and peer/teacher feedback mechanisms, but it is thinner on specific intervention strategies beyond those explicitly named in the reviews [N3-F2].

