After collaborative writing is complete, teachers support **revision, feedback, reflection, assessment, and follow-up tasks**. The evidence shows that this phase is central to learning and improvement, with technology used to strengthen feedback and reflection. The higher-education systematic review identifies **collaborative revising and editing**, **peer review and feedback**, **reflective tasks**, and **collaborative note-taking** as core instructional strategies across the writing phases [N3-F2]. It also emphasizes that technology should be intentionally aligned with pedagogical objectives to strengthen students’ collaborative engagement and academic writing development [N3-F2]. In a related context, the “Technology to Support Writing Revision” resource notes that word processing programs offer revision tools such as comments, cut/copy/paste, and drag-and-drop relocation of content [N4-R5].

Technology is used for **revision, feedback, and reflection** in several ways. The systematic review notes that platforms such as Google Docs, Moodle, Zoom, and WhatsApp can support drafting, feedback, and revision activities [N3-F2]. The same review highlights that technology-mediated collaboration can help students reflect on their writing and revise collaboratively [N3-F2]. The peer-writing and revision literature also points to self-feedback activities, which can help students understand writing requirements and grading criteria and evaluate their work against those standards [N4-R1]. This indicates that post-writing technology use extends beyond teacher feedback to include **student self-assessment and peer feedback**.

Examples of assessment, peer review, and follow-up tasks include:
- **peer review and feedback**, which is explicitly named as a core instructional strategy [N3-F2];
- **collaborative revising and editing**, which is also a core strategy [N3-F2];
- **reflective tasks**, which encourage students to think about the writing process and outcomes [N3-F2];
- **assessment through rubrics or grading criteria**, implied by the emphasis on understanding requirements and evaluation standards [N4-R1];
- **follow-up tasks** that may include further writing, reflection, or application of the collaborative work [N3-F2].

The evidence is strongest for **peer feedback, revision tools, and reflective tasks**, while the exact forms of assessment are less explicitly detailed in the provided material [N3-F2][N4-R1]. Overall, post-writing teacher roles are about helping students refine their work, reflect on the process, and translate collaborative writing into assessment and future learning [N3-F2][N4-R1].

